1
00:00:00,250 --> 00:00:06,798
A workbench for comparing the shapes of folding knives. Here, three of them, shown at their real size.

2
00:00:07,208 --> 00:00:15,352
Each knife carries its points of interest: the tip, the pivot, the junction of blade and handle, and the butt, with the axes that join them.

3
00:00:15,762 --> 00:00:19,650
Laid on top of each other, with their outlines drawn,

4
00:00:20,060 --> 00:00:24,526
and lined up on the tip, the blades can be compared directly.

5
00:00:24,936 --> 00:00:26,759
Back to rows.

6
00:00:27,169 --> 00:00:32,435
Each knife can also be turned so that its axis from pivot to butt is level,

7
00:00:32,845 --> 00:00:36,198
which makes the handles easy to compare.

8
00:00:36,608 --> 00:00:39,977
Any point can be paired with any axis.

9
00:00:40,387 --> 00:00:45,372
Here, the blade axes share a single line, with the junctions together.

10
00:00:45,782 --> 00:00:51,905
With the handles held level, the lowest point of each blade is the first to meet a flat surface.

11
00:00:52,315 --> 00:00:58,872
Tilting the handle shows how far each knife must turn before the belly of the edge comes into contact.

12
00:01:00,282 --> 00:01:04,235
Any knife in the catalogue can be added with a search.

13
00:01:05,845 --> 00:01:09,354
The four can be overlaid again, tip on tip.

14
00:01:09,764 --> 00:01:14,680
Once the screen is calibrated, every knife appears at its true size.

15
00:01:15,090 --> 00:01:23,989
Each knife was rebuilt from openly published photographs: outline traced, blade and handle separated, pivot located, scale restored.

16
00:01:24,399 --> 00:01:31,067
The workbench is meant for discovering, learning and trying things out, on knives you own or simply like.

17
00:01:31,477 --> 00:01:34,777
Suggestions are welcome.

